[Chapter "Advance Variation"]
[Line "Tal Variation (4.h4)"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation, Tal Variation"]

{White pushes the h-pawn to harass the bishop. Black stops it at once.} 1. e4 c6 {Prepares ...d5, so Black can challenge the centre with a pawn while the c8 bishop keeps its diagonal open.} 2. d4 d5 {The point of the Caro-Kann: hit e4 straight away, backed up by the c6 pawn.} 3. e5 Bf5 {Develop the bishop before ...e6.} 4. h4 {White wants h4–h5, chasing the bishop.} 4... h5 {Stop the h-pawn in its tracks. The g5 square is weakened, but White's h4 pawn is fixed too.} 5. Bd3 Bxd3 {Trade off White's best attacking bishop rather than retreat.} 6. Qxd3 e6 {Complete the pawn chain; ...c5 comes next.} 7. Bg5 Qb6 {Attacks b2 and adds pressure on d4 at the same time.} 8. Nd2 c5 {The standard break. With the queen on b6, d4 is under real pressure.} *
